    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "okay so it's like a massive matching game. but what happens if the signal is super noisy or has weird, irregular spikes that don't really fit a smooth wave? does the transform just get confused or does it somehow separate the \"truth\" from the noise?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When the signal is noisy, the Fourier Transform doesn't magically separate the \"truth\" from the noise; it shows you *both*! The noise, being random, is spread across many frequencies, so the transform will show lots of small, scattered energy peaks across the frequency spectrum. The \"truth\"—the underlying signal—will show up as distinct, strong peaks.\n\nThis is where the real work comes in, which is often done *after* the transform. You use techniques like filtering (which is just selectively cutting out certain frequency bins) to suppress the noise frequencies while keeping the dominant signal frequencies. It's a process of intelligent subtraction, not automatic discernment.",
      "source": "target",
